### =============================================
### CHAOS ENGINEERING COMPREHENSIVE DEMO SCRIPT
### =============================================
### PURPOSE: Demonstrates advanced failure simulation and debugging features
### AUDIENCE: Perfect for showcasing dashboard debugging capabilities
### SCENARIOS: Covers retry patterns, circuit breakers, cascading failures
### MONITORING: Rich diagnostic events for real-time dashboard consumption
### DURATION: Allow 10-15 minutes for full demonstration
### =============================================

### =============================================
### PHASE 1: BASELINE SYSTEM HEALTH ASSESSMENT
### =============================================

### 1. Check initial chaos engineering status
### Expected: chaos_enabled=true, active_failures=0, total_scenarios=0
GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/status

### 2. Check circuit breaker states (should be all healthy initially)
### Expected: All services show "closed" state, failure_count=0
GET http://localhost:3006/circuit-breakers

### 3. Verify all services are healthy before injecting failures
### Expected: All services return 200 OK status
GET http://localhost:3010/health

### =============================================
### PHASE 2: SCENARIO CREATION & CONFIGURATION
### =============================================

### 4. Create a comprehensive database failure scenario
### PURPOSE: Tests inventory service resilience and cascade effects
### MONITORING: Watch for database_error events in dashboard
// @name database_failure_scenario
POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Database Cascade Failure Test",
  "description": "Simulate database failures cascading through inventory and payment services with 80% failure rate",
  "failure_type": "database_error",
  "target_service": "inventory",
  "failure_rate": 0.8,
  "duration_seconds": 120,
  "enabled": true
}

### 5. Create a payment service timeout scenario
### PURPOSE: Demonstrates timeout handling and retry patterns
### MONITORING: Watch for exponential backoff retry attempts
// @name payment_timeout_scenario
POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Payment Service Timeout",
  "description": "Simulate timeout failures in payment processing with retry pattern demonstration",
  "failure_type": "timeout",
  "target_service": "payments",
  "failure_rate": 0.7,
  "duration_seconds": 90,
  "enabled": true
}

### 6. Create a network timeout scenario for additional testing
### PURPOSE: Tests network-level failures and circuit breaker triggers
### MONITORING: Observe circuit breaker state changes
// @name network_timeout_scenario
POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Network Infrastructure Timeout",
  "description": "Simulate network-level timeouts affecting multiple services simultaneously",
  "failure_type": "network_error",
  "target_service": "order-processor",
  "failure_rate": 0.6,
  "duration_seconds": 75,
  "enabled": true
}

### 7. Create a shipping cascade failure scenario
### PURPOSE: Demonstrates failure isolation and containment
### MONITORING: Track cascade propagation through service dependencies
// @name shipping_cascade_scenario
POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Multi-Service Cascade Failure",
  "description": "Test how failures cascade from shipping through the entire order pipeline at 95% failure rate",
  "failure_type": "service_unavailable",
  "target_service": "shipping",
  "failure_rate": 0.95,
  "duration_seconds": 60,
  "enabled": true
}

### =============================================
### PHASE 3: WORKFLOW EXECUTION & MONITORING
### =============================================

### 8. Execute database failure scenario
### EXPECTED WORKFLOW: Health check → Failure injection → Retry testing → Circuit breaker → Recovery
### MONITORING: Real-time [CHAOS] events in notifications UI (http://localhost:8080)
@database_scenario_id = {{database_failure_scenario.response.body.id}}

POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios/{{database_scenario_id}}/execute
Content-Type: application/json

### 9. Execute payment timeout scenario (demonstrates retry patterns)
### EXPECTED BEHAVIOR: 3 retry rounds × 3 retry policies = 9 total test cycles
### RETRY POLICIES: exponential_backoff, fixed_interval, linear_backoff
@payment_scenario_id = {{payment_timeout_scenario.response.body.id}}

POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios/{{payment_scenario_id}}/execute
Content-Type: application/json

### =============================================
### PHASE 4: LIVE SYSTEM STRESS TESTING
### =============================================

### 10. Create orders during chaos scenarios to observe real impact
### PURPOSE: See how active failures affect actual business workflows
### MONITORING: Watch order processing logs for retry attempts and failures
POST http://localhost:3006/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customer": "chaos_demo_user_1",
  "item": "pear",
  "total": 150.00
}

### 11. Create high-value order to test approval workflow under chaos
### PURPOSE: Test external event handling (approval) during system stress
### MONITORING: Observe if approval workflow is affected by chaos scenarios
POST http://localhost:3006/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customer": "chaos_demo_user_2",
  "item": "apple",
  "total": 1200.00
}

### =============================================
### PHASE 5: QUICK FAILURE INJECTION TESTS
### =============================================

### 12. Quick failure injection - Network error in order processor
### PURPOSE: Immediate failure testing without full workflow overhead
### DURATION: 20 seconds of 80% failure rate
### MONITORING: Watch circuit breakers open quickly due to high failure rate
POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/inject/network_error/order-processor
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "failure_rate": 0.8,
  "duration_seconds": 20
}

### 13. Quick failure injection - Memory error in inventory
### PURPOSE: Test memory-related failure patterns
### DURATION: 15 seconds of 60% failure rate
### MONITORING: Observe service degradation in health checks
POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/inject/memory_error/inventory
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "failure_rate": 0.6,
  "duration_seconds": 15
}

### 14. Quick failure injection - Rate limiting in payments
### PURPOSE: Simulate API rate limiting scenarios
### DURATION: 25 seconds of 90% failure rate
### MONITORING: High failure rate should trigger circuit breakers rapidly
POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/inject/rate_limited/payments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "failure_rate": 0.9,
  "duration_seconds": 25
}

### =============================================
### PHASE 6: REAL-TIME MONITORING & DIAGNOSTICS
### =============================================

### 15. Monitor circuit breaker states during active failures
### PURPOSE: Live demonstration of circuit breaker pattern in action
### EXPECTED: Should show "open" or "half-open" states with failure counts
GET http://localhost:3006/circuit-breakers

### 16. Get comprehensive diagnostic events for dashboard
### PURPOSE: Rich structured data perfect for dashboard visualization
### DATA: Events with correlation IDs, severity levels, execution times
GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/events?limit=50

### 17. Filter critical error events for alerting systems
### PURPOSE: Demonstrate filtering for high-priority dashboard alerts
### USE CASE: Critical error monitoring and alerting
GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/events?severity=error&limit=25

### 18. Get warning events for operational monitoring
### PURPOSE: Medium-priority events for operational awareness
### USE CASE: Service degradation monitoring
GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/events?severity=warning&limit=25

### 19. Get info events for detailed troubleshooting
### PURPOSE: Detailed workflow progression for debugging
### USE CASE: End-to-end workflow tracing and analysis
GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/events?severity=info&limit=30

### =============================================
### PHASE 7: CORRELATION & TRACING ANALYSIS
### =============================================

### 20. Filter events by correlation ID for end-to-end tracing
### PURPOSE: Demonstrate complete workflow tracing capabilities
### BENEFIT: Full context from scenario start to completion
### NOTE: Replace {{database_scenario_id}} with actual correlation ID from executed scenarios
GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/events?correlation_id={{database_scenario_id}}&limit=100

### 21. Get all active chaos scenarios with expiration times
### PURPOSE: Show currently running failure scenarios
### MONITORING: Track which failures are still active
GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios

### 22. Get specific scenario details for deep analysis
### PURPOSE: Detailed configuration and execution status
### USE CASE: Scenario configuration verification and debugging
@payment_scenario_id = {{payment_timeout_scenario.response.body.id}}

GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios/{{payment_scenario_id}}

### =============================================
### PHASE 8: ADVANCED FAILURE SCENARIOS
### =============================================

### 23. Execute network timeout scenario for compound testing
### PURPOSE: Layer multiple failure types for complex scenarios
### MONITORING: Observe interaction between different failure patterns
@network_scenario_id = {{network_timeout_scenario.response.body.id}}

POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios/{{network_scenario_id}}/execute
Content-Type: application/json

### 24. Execute shipping cascade scenario for dependency testing
### PURPOSE: Demonstrate failure propagation through service mesh
### MONITORING: Track cascade steps and containment effectiveness
@shipping_scenario_id = {{shipping_cascade_scenario.response.body.id}}

POST http://localhost:3010/chaos/scenarios/{{shipping_scenario_id}}/execute
Content-Type: application/json

### 25. Final system health check
### PURPOSE: Verify chaos status and active scenario count
### EXPECTED: Multiple active_failures during concurrent testing
GET http://localhost:3010/chaos/status

